<svelte:head><title>Voynich@home — A shared search for evidence</title><meta name="description" content="An open research project testing what our tools can recover, and what that tells us about the Voynich manuscript."/></svelte:head>
<div class="page">
  <section class="hero"><div><p class="eyebrow">An open invitation to a centuries-old question</p><h1>A mystery<br/>worth testing.</h1><p class="lede">The Voynich manuscript has resisted a convincing reading for centuries. Together, we can test possible explanations—and make the evidence open to everyone.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button" href="/contribute">Contribute computation <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a><a class="text-link" href="/methods">Explore the approach →</a></div><p class="small muted">Your browser. Your limits. No account required.</p></div>
  <figure class="evidence-figure" aria-label="Our research sequence"><div class="figure-top"><span>The research sequence</span><span>01 — 03</span></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">01</span><div><h3>Start with a known answer.</h3><p>Hide a message using a defined encoding. Measure whether our search can recover it.</p></div></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">02</span><div><h3>Test the explanation.</h3><p>Compare against shuffled and generated texts. Record failures as carefully as successes.</p></div></div><div class="figure-step"><span class="number">03</span><div><h3>Publish the evidence.</h3><p>Share the rules, actual output, and reproduction records. Let others examine the result.</p></div></div><figcaption class="figure-caption">A result earns attention through checks, not attractive words.</figcaption></figure></section>
  <div class="strip"><div><span class="badge">Current focus · known-message recovery</span></div><p>We are building and measuring the search before opening a manuscript campaign. A decipherment is a research outcome, never a release promise. <a href="/experiments">See the current evidence →</a></p></div>
  <section><div class="section-heading"><h2>There is more than one<br/>way to help.</h2><p class="small muted">Computation, expertise, and careful criticism.</p></div><div class="cards"><article class="card"><div class="card-number">01 / VOLUNTEERS</div><h3>Give a little computing time.</h3><p>Run approved work in your browser. Set your intensity, pause whenever you like, and see contributions after they are checked.</p><a href="/contribute">See how participation works →</a></article><article class="card"><div class="card-number">02 / RESEARCHERS</div><h3>Make a testable proposal.</h3><p>Inspect the assumptions and source material. Propose a bounded experiment with clear comparisons and stopping conditions.</p><a href="/methods">Read the research methods →</a></article><article class="card"><div class="card-number">03 / CONTRIBUTORS</div><h3>Build something reproducible.</h3><p>Improve the Rust kernel, browser experience, language resources, or operational checks. The source and contracts are open.</p><a href="/downloads">Build the project locally →</a></article></div></section>
  <section class="prose" style="margin-top:75px"><p class="eyebrow">The manuscript</p><h2>One object. Many unresolved questions.</h2><p class="lede">The illustrated Voynich manuscript is held at Yale’s Beinecke Library. Its writing, illustrations, and structure offer clues, but no proposed reading has established a broadly accepted solution.</p><p>Our contribution is a public way to test precise claims. More computers help when a useful search method exists; they cannot make an unrestricted search practical.</p><a class="text-link" href="https://beinecke.library.yale.edu/collections/highlights/voynich-manuscript">Visit the manuscript’s collection page ↗</a></section>
